<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Nöteborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1323">1323</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Orekhovets · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-novgorodian-wars">Swedish–Novgorodian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Nöteborg</a> was a peace concluded in 1323 at the fortress of Nöteborg (Orekhovets) at the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Novgorod Republic. It drew the first agreed border across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, leaving the western part with Sweden and the east with Novgorod. The line shaped the later divide between Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-noteborg.jpg" alt="Treaty of Nöteborg" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velivieras · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A4hkin%C3%A4saaren_rauhan_raja_1323_-_Treaty_of_N%C3%B6teborg_1323.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the mid-thirteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> consolidated its rule over Tavastia and expanded into western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> to control the waterways between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, on which the Novgorod Republic relied for its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. After Sweden gained full control of the western outlet of the Vuoksi River in 1293, it founded the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viborg</a>.</p><p>Swedish attempts to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a> and to hold the fortress of Landskrone, at the mouth of the Okhta, were repulsed in 1294 and 1300, and the Novgorodians demolished Landskrone in 1301. In 1322 the Swedes again tried to control Kexholm, while the Novgorodians unsuccessfully tried to drive them out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viborg</a>. The next year the Novgorodians built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Oreshek</a>, where the two sides agreed to end hostilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Oreshek</a>, called Nöteborg in Swedish, on 12 August 1323. The Russian text names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-of-moscow">Yury of Moscow</a>, then prince of Novgorod, the mayor Varfolomey Yuryevich and the chiliarch Avram. The seven-year-old Swedish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-eriksson">Magnus Eriksson</a> was represented by the lords Erik Turesson and Hemming Ødgislason, the castellan of Viborg Peter Jonsson and the priest Waemundus, and two merchants from Gotland represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p>The border was defined by a list of 18 points. The southern part near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viborg</a>, the most important, was set by twelve points and divided the Karelian Isthmus in half, running north along the Sestra and Volchya rivers to the Vuoksi and on to Särkilahti on Lake Saimaa. Novgorod ceded the parishes of Savilahti, Jääski and Äyräpää to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p>The northern six points crossed sparsely populated country and were defined vaguely; historians have debated them for generations. Nineteenth-century historians took the border to run north through Kainuu, while Jalmari Jaakkola argued in 1925 for a line towards the Gulf of Bothnia. John Lind proposed in the 1990s that the treaty set two borders with common land between them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> on both sides had no say in the treaty. The northern border later became a centre of contention, and within five years Swedish colonists were moving into northern Ostrobothnia. Because the line cut through the Karelian heartland, Novgorod relied on Lithuanian service princes, whose reliance on local contributions did not make its rule popular.</p><p>By 1337 a local uprising at Korelsky Gorodok called for Swedish intervention and led to the killing of Russian merchants and other Christians. After two years of fighting the treaty was renewed in 1339 with an added clause on fugitive Karelians. In 1348 Magnus launched another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> region and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Oreshek</a>, but the Swedes could not hold it. The border did not change until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-teusina">Treaty of Teusina</a> in 1595.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Nöteborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Medieval Swedish warfare was directed east: expeditions traditionally called the Swedish crusades extended rule over Finland, Torgils Knutsson founded Vyborg castle in 1293, and the <b>Treaty of Nöteborg</b> of 1323 fixed a first border with Novgorod.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Conflict with Sweden over Karelia and the Neva ended for a time with the <b>Treaty of Nöteborg</b> of 1323, which drew the first border between them, after Novgorod built the fortress of Oreshek at the source of the Neva.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod had settled its conflict with Sweden in 1323 in the <b>Treaty of Nöteborg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-3">3 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Nöteborg</b> in 1323 divided Karelia between Sweden and Novgorod, a border that shaped the later divide between Lutheran Finland and Orthodox Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelians">Karelians</a></li></ul></section>
